This allows to show the senders:  xzcat gmane.linux.kernel\(3\)\(1\).xz | grep -a -i '^From:'

Basice sorting: cat file | sort 
Sorting numerically: cat file | sort -n
Sorting a specific key: cat file | sort -k 2 (Why do we need 2?)
with specific separator: cat file | sort -t 't' -k 3
elminating duplicates: cat file | sort -u


My solution
xzcat gmane.linux.kernel\(3\)\(1\).xz | grep -a -i '^From:' | sort

xzcat gmane.linux.kernel\(3\)\(1\).xz | grep -a -i '^From:' | sort | uniq -c | sort -n | tail -10

xzcat gmane.linux.kernel\(3\)\(1\).xz | grep -a -i '^From:' | sort | uniq -u



Proposed solution
xzcat gmane.linux.kernel\(3\)\(1\).xz | grep -a -i '^From:' | grep -a -i -o -E '[a-z0-9]+@[a-z0-9]+\.[a-z]{2,}' | sort

xzcat gmane.linux.kernel\(3\)\(1\).xz | grep -a -i '^From:' | grep -a -i -o -E '[a-z0-9]+@[a-z0-9]+\.[a-z]{2,}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -n -k 1 | tail -n -10

xzcat gmane.linux.kernel\(3\)\(1\).xz | grep -a -i '^From:' | grep -a -i -o -E '[a-z0-9]+@[a-z0-9]+\.[a-z]{2,}' | sort | uniq -u



